g7 leaders meeting in southern germany  have agreed to invest hundreds of  billions of dollars in global  infrastructure the move aims to help  developing countries and counter china's  drive to exert influence in emerging  economies through large-scale  investments but it's russia's war in  ukraine that's dominating the three-day  meeting of the world's seven richest  democracies including the global  economic impact of moscow's invasion  they are meeting in the tranquil  surroundings of the bavarian alps  the top of the agenda is of course the  war in ukraine and the worldwide fallout  we've just tried to overcome a global  pandemic and it shook the global economy  and then as the global economy was just  recovering  russia's vicious attack on ukraine  happened  driving prices up  everywhere from food to energy  and casting deep uncertainty  especially in the most fragile countries  as host german chancellor olaf schultz  says the world is facing economic  difficulties just as big as the problems  of the 1970s when the g7 was starting  out  falling growth rates rising inflation  raw material shortages and supply chain  disruptions these are no small  challenges and we must share the  responsibility  u.s president joe biden announced a 600  billion dollar g7 initiative to boost  infrastructure in developing countries  with the aim of countering china's  growing global influence  but developing countries often lack the  essential infrastructure to help  navigate global shocks like a pandemic  so they feel the impacts more acutely  and they have a harder time recovering  in our deeply connected world  that's not just a humanitarian concern  it's an economic and a security concern  for all of us  tight security has so far prevented any  unrest at the summit but protesters have  gathered on the fringes  mainly climate change campaigners and  activists opposing germany's plans for a  big rise in defence spending  the leaders are considering more  measures to punish russia for the war in  ukraine the u.s canada japan and britain  have all announced a ban on imports of  russian gold the overall message is one  of unity in the face of aggression  let's cross over to our political  correspondent julia saldelli she's in  garmisch-potten near the summit venue  julia ukraine's president is expected to  address the g7 meeting there today are  the leaders gathered there united in  their approach to the war in ukraine  yes unity is the message that the  leaders here have been wanting to to  portray uh already yesterday but also  today when they're going to speak to  ukrainian president zielenski  they are united in criticizing russia  for its attack against ukraine which has  been defined as a brutal or uh  other similar adjectives that are have  been really critical against russia but  also in their support towards ukraine  and uh they are trying to shore the  support not only with words but also  with practical plans for example there  are discussions about possibly  implementing a ban on gold imports from  russia  gold imports that are a big source of  income from russia and are mostly  exported to g7 countries but also there  are talks about implementing a price cap  on  oil exports from russia and these are  all things that the leaders may want to  present to zelensky today and they  really want to show the ukrainian  president that the g7 countries are  there also in the long term  now helping the world deal with climate  change is another major topic for the g7  that's been the case in the past as well  how are the leaders there approaching  that  climate change has taken a bit of a back  seat now that the war in ukraine has  been the dominating topic at the g7 but  we shouldn't forget that climate change  is actually connected in some way to  russia's aggression in ukraine because a  lot of g7 countries now have the urgent  need to make themselves independent of  russian fossil fuels and they have to do  that quickly and it is an opportunity  for them to actually pursue uh faster  and bigger development of renewable  energies in in the future and that is  something that chancellor olaf schultz  also wants to bring home he has a plan  he has an idea to create a climate club  of willing countries who can be an  example and show leadership in terms of  building up renewable energy and  promoting sustainability and that is  something that is also really on the  minds of the protesters here at g7  climate has been one of the main topics  i spoke to many of them and they really  want the g7 to take concrete and fast  action in fighting climate change  julia thank you so much our  correspondent julia saudelli there in  garnish pardon care  for some more analysis let's bring in  stefan meister here he's head of the  program on international order and  democracy at the german council on  foreign relations stefan thanks for  being with us what do you make of the  proposals put forth by g7 leaders for  countering russian aggression in ukraine  and softening the economic fallout from  the war  i think we have reached a limit of  sanctions um from the from the western  side so now we are discussing gold uh i  think which which will have an impact on  the russian economy but as long as  russia still can sell sale oil and gas  globally as long as india and china and  other big countries are  buying russian gas and the russian even  the russian welfare fund is growing  i don't see that putin will will make  here any any compromise i think he will  not make any compromises anyway because  he wants ukraine to get under control  but i think i i think this is first of  all about unity this this summit but i i  don't think it will change uh russian  policy at the moment  the war in ukraine stefan is a defining  moment for the west which the g7  embodies to some degree how severely do  you see the west's influence in the  world being tested as a consequence of  this war  i think it is impressive how united  western countries are  g7 is somehow back yeah it was a it was  a declining format but i think western  democracies understand that they have to  coordinate and to cooperate more in the  light of the russian war at the same  time we can also see the limits we can  see that other countries like india like  china but also brazil south africa they  do not follow western countries on  sanctions  so we see a changing order  which is neither unipolan or bipolar  it's multipolar  and yeah and we can we can see that but  other major countries neither follow  russia nor follow the g7  you mentioned uh india and south africa  they're among a number of other  countries including indonesia and  senegal that have been invited to attend  the g7 this year  they are represented there what role are  those countries playing at that g7  i think g7 understand that they have to  engage with these countries  there is a growing axis or you can call  it between china and and russia as  authoritarian states uh and you have  countries like india which are  democracies but you also have other  countries who are  partly  democracies i think there is a growing  understanding that you have to engage  with major asian african and south  american countries as  g7 as western countries to um yeah to to  react to to challenges in in the global  world and that they need a place also on  the table to discuss major challenges  like the russian war in in ukraine and  the consequences of this war for global  economy and and food security  you said that you see a  multi-polar world emerging  where do you see  the the west particularly the united  states and europe has it their  importance been being is it being  diminished in this multi-polar world  that you see emerging  so it's the end of the u.s unipolar  world that means it is a decline also of  u.s power in global politics  europeans have to invest in in security  they have to become more independent  more more autonomous also on security  issues from the us who is not able and  willing anymore to to play the the  global police um  so you can say in the end this war  um strengthened g7 strengthen western  democracies in their cooperation but it  has shown also the limits of these  countries um and we can see that other  major players like china india indonesia  are becoming more important so you are  you can argue it's it's a diminishing of  western powers but at the same time if  they are united they are still strong  and they can make a difference  stefan thank you very much for talking  with us out with stephen meister from  the german council on foreign relations  here in berlin  thank you
 

